WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:04.403
Recessions usually mean job losses.

00:00:05.502 --> 00:00:07.640
And when a recession begins to end,

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.173
we typically see jobs
start to open up again.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.380
After all, that's what happened

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.390
after the great recession in 2008.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.920
Notably women made up a
large percentage of those

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:19.030
who entered the workforce

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.690
during that economic recovery period.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.620
After the great financial crisis in 2008,

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:27.050
because family finances were so strained,

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.860
a lot of women entered the workforce

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.100
to support their families

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.340
when they hadn't been doing so before.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:34.380
And at the same time,

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.920
we had a really, really
strong labor market,

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.020
which allowed women to enter
who hadn't been doing so.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.960
Up until early 2020, women were
making gains for, you know,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.910
the prior five years.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:48.350
Women's labor force
participation had been growing

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.090
over the past 60 years.

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:52.060
In fact, things were going so well

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.420
that in January 2020, 2 months

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.380
before the Coronavirus
pandemic shut down the US,

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.510
women had outpaced men
as the majority of the

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:02.000
workforce for the first time in a decade.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.220
This is a really good thing

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:04.860
because this is the
future of the workforce.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.853
We are increasingly becoming
a services oriented economy.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.750
But all of that progress was wiped out

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.290
when the pandemic forced everyone home,

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:18.150
disproportionately affecting
women around the world.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.370
More than 12 million women lost their jobs

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:22.840
during the pandemic and
over four and a half million

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.728
have not returned to the workforce.

00:01:24.728 --> 00:01:26.720
The impact of COVID is so severe

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.750
that it set the rate of US
female workforce participation

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.660
back more than 30 years

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:34.630
to the same rate it was in April 1987.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:37.360
So while the 2008 recession
recovery may have worked out

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.230
for many women, this time,

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:40.750
women are finding themselves burnt out

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.372
from juggling unemployment
or working full-time remotely

00:01:43.372 --> 00:01:45.230
with domestic roles.

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:47.850
Many have even decided to give
up their careers entirely to

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:51.550
tend to at-home unpaid labor
and their own personal health.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:55.970
The burden of caregiving
with schools and daycare

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.807
still not fully open in
some parts of the country

00:01:58.807 --> 00:02:01.852
is an added burden, especially on mothers.

00:02:01.852 --> 00:02:04.010
So as businesses reopen

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:05.830
and employers are hiring again,

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:08.140
many women aren't necessarily coming back.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:11.070
In a crisis that some
economists are calling the first

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.203
female recession.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.490
Out in California two
of the best mechanics

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.300
working on Link Trainer planes are women.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.620
It used to be during recessions

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.830
that women actually entered the workforce

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:24.160
to take care of their families.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.320
We saw the same after World War Two.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.510
And so this was a really unique situation

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.560
in that, rather than joining the workforce

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:31.680
to support their families,

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.890
they left the workforce
to support their families.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:35.300
According to data from McKinsey,

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.740
more than double the number
of mothers found themselves

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.910
taking on an additional five
hours of daily domestic work

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:42.740
compared to their male counterparts.

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:44.760
Much of this can be
attributed to gender norms

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.460
across societies with women
bearing more of the burden

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:48.293
at home.

00:02:48.293 --> 00:02:50.470
We know that when
there's a crisis at home,

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:53.240
whether that's a pandemic
or a family crisis

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.350
or a crisis with a child themselves,

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:59.860
it's often women that are the
ones to take on that care.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.360
And so when we had these school closures

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.990
and daycare closures,
it naturally was women

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:07.520
that left the workforce at a faster pace.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.110
What's different about this particular

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.960
recession is that it also affected women

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.830
across all pay levels
from minimum wage workers

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:15.610
to high level executives.

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:17.750
I spoke with a woman
for some of my reporting

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.290
who was a top executive at
Facebook who left her job

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:21.270
for childcare.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:24.140
When you're at such a high
executive role like that,

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:26.070
you can't just go and
start working at a bar,

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:27.720
which is really where the jobs are open.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.750
And when you have a competitive resume,

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.200
it takes more time to reenter.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.050
And so she was expecting it
could take up to six months

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:36.690
or more to kind of get the
role that she was looking for.

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:37.640
Other women who chose

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:40.130
to stay in the workforce say
they were barely hanging on

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.429
while balancing multiple full-time roles.

00:03:42.429 --> 00:03:45.470
Reshma Saujani is a CEO
of the organization,

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:46.440
Girls Who Code.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:49.230
At the height of the pandemic,
she found herself overwhelmed

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:52.330
with juggling household
chores, caring for her sons,

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:54.500
and working on her nonprofit remotely.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.560
I was doing the homeschooling
while I was running

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:58.393
my organization.

00:03:58.393 --> 00:04:00.210
In between, I was doing the laundry.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:02.120
You know what I mean,
doing the dishes, right?

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.883
Ordering whatever I needed on Amazon.

00:04:03.883 --> 00:04:07.460
And so by the end of the
day, I was just done.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:10.030
You know, I got COVID
and it barely registered.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:11.224
You know, my liver failed.

00:04:11.224 --> 00:04:14.780
I had acne on my face as
if I was like 16 years old.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.570
And it wasn't just me.

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:17.930
In response to the pandemic,

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:19.760
Reshma is proposing a Marshall plan

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.150
to better support working
moms, including wage equity,

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:25.660
paid family leave, and
work retraining programs

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.814
for women return from maternity leave.

00:04:27.814 --> 00:04:28.647
So, you know,

00:04:28.647 --> 00:04:30.390
moms are not America's social safety net.

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:31.630
We're one of the few countries

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:34.000
that doesn't offer a paternity allowance.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.570
That's come today in the
form of a child tax credit.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:40.650
Today, the expanded
child tax credit is here.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.100
But that was a revolutionary idea.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:46.103
That's been met with a
tremendous amount of resistance.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.990
As a mother, a single mother,
like you need to be there

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.740
for everything for your child.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:55.940
I was working at 311.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:57.490
I got a call.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:59.810
It was like an emergency call.

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:02.700
And my supervisor's like,
you can't use your phone.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.870
So if people need you,

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:06.640
they have to call like the office

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:10.780
and you would get connected
through your supervisor.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:14.041
My child ended up at the
hospital and I'm in the middle

00:05:14.041 --> 00:05:16.987
of the floor, at her
desk answering the phone,

00:05:17.829 --> 00:05:21.270
I'm instantly devastated,
but I'm also embarrassed,

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:25.620
frustrated, aggravated that
I had to get this phone call

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.150
like this, in front of
this woman, standing here

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:29.420
in the midst of everybody.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:33.760
I ended up losing the job
because when you're out,

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.650
you have to call in and tell
them kind of what's going on

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:40.390
or the reason for your being out.

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:44.010
And at that time, I couldn't
even talk without crying.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.130
That really kind of
threw me off as a person.

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:48.300
Burnout isn't just something

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.380
that happens at work.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:51.920
It's something that happens
when you're, you know,

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:53.480
a domestic childcare provider.

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:56.340
And so if you're trying to
do both of those things,

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:57.880
people's mental health suffers.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:05:59.390
This stress is compounded

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:02.050
during the pandemic with one
piece of research stating

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:02.930
that working women

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:06.580
have reported increased
levels of depression by 83%,

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:09.020
compared to 36% of working men.

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:11.270
Crucially, this can
impact a woman's ability

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:12.840
to progress in her career.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:15.080
And when women are
underrepresented at work,

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:16.640
companies are less incentivized

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:18.220
to provide them the support they need.

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:21.440
Women do a tremendous
amount of unpaid labor,

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:25.760
almost 86% of the household
chores and the childcare rearing

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.470
is done by women.

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:30.650
And so you simply can't raise
your hand for that promotion.

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:31.960
You can't start that business.

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:33.950
You can't take on that nursing degree

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:35.150
that you've always dreamed about

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:37.580
because you have so
much happening at home.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.520
And this is why we have
enormous levels of poverty

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:41.690
when it comes to women of color,

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:43.776
because they're in low paying jobs

00:06:43.776 --> 00:06:45.220
and they're not getting
the support that they need

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:46.340
to thrive.

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:50.090
So I lost my job, stopped paying rent.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:52.680
We went to live in the shelter.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.140
One of the things that I did

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:57.440
with a lot of my free time
was volunteer at church.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:59.290
We wanted to show the scriptures,

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:01.475
like, so if people
didn't have their Bible,

00:07:01.475 --> 00:07:03.860
they could just look up on the screen.

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:07.630
I figured out what device to
use and what program to run it.

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:10.980
I had some help, but a
lot of it is me running it

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:13.126
and doing it and implementing it.

00:07:13.126 --> 00:07:14.880
One of my friends,

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:19.350
she saw that I had a
good hold to technology.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:20.730
On her friend's recommendation,

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:24.350
Tanya soon applies and gets
accepted into a coding bootcamp.

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:27.272
It's a program where
you don't pay upfront,

00:07:27.272 --> 00:07:30.180
but it's also like an unpaid thing.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:32.370
So there was no income coming from there.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:35.090
So I'm in Pursuit, struggling.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:36.840
Things are moving very, very quickly.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:38.200
When it comes to the coronavirus,

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.250
I'm stating the obvious to everyone here.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.400
Covid hits.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:45.960
I had to shift my whole life
to make sure that I supported

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.340
and covered my children.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.730
I have to leave early every day from class

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:51.010
so I could get my daughter

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:52.810
and so that she could go into the shelter.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:55.422
We're told to stay indoors, stay in place.

00:07:55.422 --> 00:08:00.422
But I couldn't because I still
needed to do the classes.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:03.780
And I was having issues
with the wifi they gave me.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:06.050
So I would go to my church.

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:08.460
I think the thing that broke so many of us

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.850
and it still breaks me now is

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:13.180
it didn't seem like anybody cared.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:17.440
It didn't seem like anybody
was saying, gosh, the moms,

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.020
you know, what are we gonna do?

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.240
Because this country
doesn't function without us,

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:25.580
but we just kind of ate
it up and kept going.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:27.450
Still many women found themselves

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:29.900
without any other
options, but to persevere.

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:32.890
I had a lot of outside stressors.

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:35.750
There's a lot of assignments
that I wasn't able to complete.

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:37.950
I get through the program at Pursuit,

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:41.690
I'm able to move on to the next steps

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:45.213
with my fellow students in the class.

00:08:45.213 --> 00:08:48.100
And I'm not found job ready,

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:50.820
but I still started applying to jobs

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:52.500
just like everybody else.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:56.266
One of the alumni posted a job position.

00:08:56.266 --> 00:08:57.883
I applied.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:02.290
I got hired at The Knot Worldwide.

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:04.893
When I got the job, I was crying.

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:06.733
I was crying.

00:09:06.733 --> 00:09:09.340
My boss, nobody knew my situation.

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:10.340
I didn't tell them like,

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:12.470
that's not something you tell
people you're interviewing.

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:15.260
No, you just go to see
confidence and competence.

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:16.790
That's all you're going to see.

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:18.520
While Tanya found success in the end.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:21.410
This isn't always the case for
other working moms struggling

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:23.350
to find a work-life balance.

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:26.000
And there's still plenty to
be done from both the private

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.780
and public sectors to better
support working women.

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:30.885
And then we ask ourselves, you know,

00:09:30.885 --> 00:09:31.990
why do we have a declining birth rate?

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:35.020
Well, the reality is it's
too expensive to have kids.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:37.000
And so it's not that people
don't wanna have them,

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:38.650
it's that they can't.

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:40.410
A lot of companies pride themselves

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:42.810
on offering paid parental leave.

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:44.860
Some of these companies
offer moms six months,

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:45.820
dad three months.

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:46.840
Why?

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:48.730
Or when you take, when you're a guy

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:52.090
and you actually take the
parental leave, it's frowned upon.

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:53.740
We've seen companies like LinkedIn

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:55.940
and other tech companies kind of offering

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:57.600
alternative childcare.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:58.790
Last summer, for example,

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:00.720
some companies offered summer camps.

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:02.410
And then in the public sector,

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:04.350
we've seen Biden making a big push

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:06.770
for what we call soft infrastructure,

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:09.030
which would be universal childcare

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:11.366
for children ages three and four,

00:10:11.366 --> 00:10:14.920
as well as childcare subsidies
for those from zero to five.

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:16.600
The current child tax credit

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:19.010
that was passed during the
pandemic offers families

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:22.050
up to $300 in monthly payments per child.

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:24.890
As part of her Marshall Plan
for Moms' proposal, Reshma

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.500
is calling for these credits
to be extended indefinitely

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:28.770
among other benefits.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:30.490
Making the child tax credit permanent,

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:32.180
getting affordable childcare, you know,

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:34.370
passing paid leave once and for all.

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:36.440
The second piece is
really working, you know,

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:38.650
on the private sector and making sure

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:43.060
that we build a work environment
that works for parents

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:45.590
offering parental leave,
offering flexibility,

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:47.640
finally getting equal pay for equal work.

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:51.230
With so many things going on,

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:54.120
we have to choose what our
children over our jobs?

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:55.900
Like, we're capable.

00:10:55.900 --> 00:11:00.000
And if we're paid enough for
the value that we do bring,

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.660
we can actually be able to do a bit more.

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:04.040
Even as Congress makes moves

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:06.489
towards financial assistance
for working mothers,

00:11:06.489 --> 00:11:08.450
there are also many other issues

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:11.750
preventing women from fully
returning to work in person.

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:14.480
From sexual discrimination to harassment,

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:16.770
to microaggression at the workplace.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:20.060
And then it's the men who have
face time with their bosses

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:23.030
and are getting the promotions
and are being noticed.

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:24.680
If employers really want to create

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:27.000
a greater culture of belonging,

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:30.410
it does require addressing systems.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:31.750
And that takes money.

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:33.860
COVID has given us this opportunity

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:35.380
to look at our society and say,

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:37.930
you know what I want to
build it back better, right?

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:41.343
Like this is broken and it
is okay to acknowledge it.

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:45.340
It's not just for moms, but it's for dads.

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:47.080
It's for millennials who
don't want to have kids,

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:48.424
it's for everybody to be like, uh uh.

00:11:48.424 --> 00:11:52.240
We are actually going to
redesign the workplace

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:55.170
so that it works for us.

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:56.853
And we have to recognize that we have

00:11:56.853 --> 00:12:00.390
a once in a lifetime
opportunity to do that.

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:01.590
So while financial help

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:03.870
and flexible work
accommodations can be helpful

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:05.810
in supporting the female workforce,

00:12:05.810 --> 00:12:08.870
the solution really begins
at home on a societal level.

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:11.400
We still have inequality at home.

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:14.330
We're never gonna have
equality in the workplace.

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:15.910
We didn't go through hell and back

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:18.630
to stay exactly where
we're at when this started.

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:21.820
Let's use this opportunity
to fix things for once.

00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:25.033
Let's finish this fight,
like once and for all.

